# auditd rules — memory-read tripwire, syscall-level (WS-7)
#
# The poller (pseudex/tripwire.py, PSEUDEX_ROLE=tripwire) catches a ptrace-attach
# (gdb/gcore) and a held-open /proc/<pid>/mem fd (dd/a carver, while the slow multi-MB
# read is in flight). These auditd rules close the one gap the poller cannot see: a
# single `process_vm_readv` leaves no fd and no ptrace-attach — it is a syscall, so only
# a syscall-level sensor catches it.
#
# Install (on the CUSTOMER host, which must run auditd):
#   cp audit-pseudex.rules /etc/audit/rules.d/pseudex.rules && augenrules --load
# Read the hits (uid/pid/comm/exe of the reader):
#   ausearch -k pseudex_mem -i
#
# HONEST LIMIT: auditd runs in the host kernel the customer's root controls. A prepared
# root can `auditctl -D` (flush rules), stop auditd, or read via /dev/mem / a kernel
# module — bypassing this. It is a TRIPWIRE + DETERRENT: it forces the attacker to first
# disable the auditor (an act that is itself conspicuous and stops the tripwire's
# heartbeat, which the vendor sees), not a guarantee. Pair it with the poller (heartbeats
# + reports to the vendor in real time) so evidence leaves the box before a local scrub.

# ptrace of any kind (PTRACE_ATTACH/SEIZE/PEEKTEXT) — gdb, gcore, a debugger.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S ptrace -k pseudex_mem
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S ptrace -k pseudex_mem

# process_vm_readv — the fd-less, ptrace-less cross-process read (the poller's blind spot).
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S process_vm_readv -k pseudex_mem
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S process_vm_readv -k pseudex_mem

# Direct physical-memory paths a root may reach for. Watching the device nodes catches an
# open of them; a read of /proc/<pid>/mem is caught by the poller's fd scan.
-w /dev/mem -p r -k pseudex_mem
-w /dev/kmem -p r -k pseudex_mem
-w /proc/kcore -p r -k pseudex_mem
